  1989). The error: a truncation with no bound on what was dropped is called gold because nothing better was
  affordable, and it fails without warning when the truncation is bad.
- DeepMind's FermiNet (Pfau, Spencer and co-authors, 2020) and AlphaFold (2021). The error: the answer is
  fitted by a neural network. It carries no proof and no derivation, and it explains nothing.
- Pekeris (1958), Drake, Schwartz (2006) on helium; Nakatsuji's free-complement method. The error: dozens of
